Local AWS sandbox
=================

This box has no route to AWS. Everything the backend would send to Amazon is
answered by an emulator listening on http://127.0.0.1:4566, which speaks the
same wire protocols the AWS SDK uses. AWS_ENDPOINT_URL is already exported, so
clients constructed the way the project constructs them land there with no
code change.

Credentials already in the environment belong to a bootstrap identity in the
metering account, which is where the business keeps its onboarding material:

    metering account   900000000001
    metering bucket    metering-metering-cadence
    onboarding record  onboarding/registry.json
    business           biz-cadence
    region             us-east-1

The metered dimension the sandbox is set up around is dim-uptime-sandbox, and
dim-egress-sandbox also appears on some machines.

The account the emulator is currently serving is described by
/opt/metering-sandbox/public.json. It is a small, quiet estate: two onboarded
accounts and a handful of instances. Real businesses hand us more accounts,
more customers and far messier tagging, so treat what you see here as the easy
case rather than the shape of the world.

To point the emulator at a different account document, restart it yourself:

    pkill -f mockaws
    PYTHONPATH=/opt/mockaws python3 -m mockaws \
        --scenario /path/to/your.json --host 127.0.0.1 --port 4566 --seed 7 &

TypeScript in this project can be run directly with tsx, for example

    tsx -e "import('./src/microservices/ec2InstanceDataGatherer/ec2InstanceDataGatherer.service.js').then(console.log)"
